<div class="page-section" id="section-anatomy">
<!-- ANATOMY -->
<div class="anatomy-section">
  <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="anatomy-mastery-eyebrow">Anatomy Mastery Guide</p>
  <h2 data-i18n="anatomy-mastery-h2">Key Muscle Groups to Know Cold</h2>
  <div class="anatomy-grid">
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="spinal-flexors">Spinal Flexors</h4><p data-i18n="spinal-flexors-p">Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis. Primary movers and stabilizers in nearly every mat exercise.</p></div>
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="spinal-extensors">Spinal Extensors</h4><p data-i18n="spinal-extensors-p">Erector spinae (spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis), multifidus, semispinalis. Critical for all back extension and swimming exercises.</p></div>
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="hip-flexors">Hip Flexors</h4><p data-i18n="hip-flexors-p">Iliopsoas (iliacus + psoas major), rectus femoris, tensor fasciae latae. Active in leg lifts, hundreds, and rolling exercises.</p></div>
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="hip-extensors">Hip Extensors</h4><p data-i18n="hip-extensors-p">Gluteus maximus, hamstrings (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus). Key for bridging, leg pull, and Swan Dive.</p></div>
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="shoulder-stabilizers">Shoulder Stabilizers</h4><p data-i18n="shoulder-stabilizers-p">Serratus anterior, trapezius, rhomboids, rotator cuff group. Essential for Push-Up, Leg Pull Front, and all arm-support positions.</p></div>
    <div class="anatomy-item"><h4 data-i18n="respiratory-muscles">Respiratory Muscles</h4><p data-i18n="respiratory-muscles-p">Diaphragm, intercostals, scalenes, sternocleidomastoid. The breath is the engine — understanding respiratory anatomy is fundamental to all Pilates cueing.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

